    "updated_at": "2026-05-05T22:31:22Z",
    "body": "I don't think this can be rolled into `CanvasState.reset()`. Resizing the canvas requires setting attributes on the `<canvas>` element, and I don't think it's kosher to let a `CanvasRenderingContext` method alter the DOM. Maybe we could add `HTMLCanvasElement.getDevicePixelContentBoxSize()` for this purpose:\n\n```\nconst [w, h] = canvas.getDevicePixelContentBoxSize();\ncanvas.width = width;\ncanvas.height = height;\n```\n\nModifying the `width` or `height` of a `<canvas>` element will trigger an implicit reset of the canvas ([spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/canvas.html#concept-canvas-set-bitmap-dimensions)). So the net effect would be pretty much the same, but I think this would be a more web-y API shape.\n\nKeep in mind that `ResizeObserver` with the `device-pixel-content-box` option is the *only* existing API that exposes pixel-snapped geometry, and it's only implemented in chromium. The above proposed API would also expose pixel-snapped geometry, and it's not clear to me how the other browser vendors might feel about this.\n\nHaving said all that, it's worth pointing out that adding an API along these lines might be more convenient, but it is *not* optimally correct...\n\nThe `device-pixel-content-box` option was added based on strenuous requests from canvas developers, who are sensitive to the risk of a FOUC when resizing the canvas. We designed the new canvas `paint` event with this in mind, so that it would be possible to never be a frame late while also never needing to paint redundantly. Currently, our recommended best practice is:\n\n```\nfunction update_state() {\n  // update application/scene state but do not draw\n  if (canvas_should_be_redrawn) {\n    canvas.requestPaint();\n  }\n  // update state in a continuous loop\n  requestAnimationFrame(update_state);\n}\nrequestAnimationFrame(update_state);\n\ncanvas.addEventListener('paint', evt => {\n  // canvas draw code\n});\n\nnew ResizeObserver(entries => {\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\n    entry.target.width = entry.devicePixelContentBoxSize[0].inlineSize;\n    entry.target.height = entry.devicePixelContentBoxSize[0].blockSize;\n    canvas.requestPaint();\n  });\n}).observe(canvas, {box: 'device-pixel-content-box'});\n```\n\nThe above code is optimal: canvas drawing code is only executed when strictly necessary, and there is never a FOUC due to canvas resizing. `drawElementImage` integrates very cleanly into this flow: in addition to the existing `requestPaint()` invocations, any change to a child element of the canvas will also trigger a (necessary) `paint` event.\n\nAdapting this to `HTMLCanvasElement.getDevicePixelContentBoxSize()` might look like this:\n\n```\nfunction update_state() {\n  const [w, h] = canvas.getDevicePixelContentBoxSize();\n  if (canvas.width != w || canvas.height != h) {\n    canvas.width = w;\n    canvas.height = h;\n    canvas.requestPaint();\n  }\n  // update application/scene state but do not draw\n  if (canvas_should_be_redrawn) {\n    canvas.requestPaint();\n  }\n  // update state in a continuous loop\n  requestAnimationFrame(update_state);\n}\nrequestAnimationFrame(update_state);\n\ncanvas.addEventListener('paint', evt => {\n  // canvas draw code\n});\n```\n\nThis is a bit simpler than the `ResizeObserver` version, but there is a risk of FOUC if the CSS box size of the canvas changes *after* `update_state()` runs. Given the feedback we received from canvas developers around `ResizeObserver`, it's not clear to me how attractive this would be.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
