    "created_at": "2026-05-06T01:37:10Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-05-06T01:37:10Z",
    "body": "> I don't think it's kosher to let a CanvasRenderingContext method alter the DOM.\n\nFWIW, the `OffscreenCanvas` interface already sets the attached placeholder `<canvas>`'s attributes when setting its `width` or `height` properties. But having it on the canvas directly would also allow it to be used in other context types, so why not.\n\n> and it's only implemented in chromium\n\nGecko also has it. It's only missing in Webkit.\n\n> Adapting this to HTMLCanvasElement.getDevicePixelContentBoxSize() might look like this:\n> [...]\n\nCan't the `canvas.width = w; canvas.height = h` lines be set every time during the `onpaint` event, so that you're up to date?\nThis would make the state update loop only call `requestPaint()`, for apps that need a state update loop. For simpler cases that only need to handle user events they might not even need that rAF loop at all and only call `requestPaint` from event handlers.\n\n> but there is a risk of FOUC if the CSS box size of the canvas changes after update_state() runs.\n\nThat's a real drawback to this proposal, I must admit I hadn't foresee it. This makes me think now that this would actually be less useful for non HiC cases, and might even be a footgun...  \nIn that case, what about exposing it inside the `onpaint` event instead? (and maybe move that issue to HiC's repo).",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
